class:: PNCC
summary:: Power-normalized cepstra with medium-time noise suppression
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/GFCC, Classes/MFCC2

description::
Computes a native online adaptation of Kim and Stern's PNCC (IEEE/ACM TASLP 2016, DOI 10.1109/TASLP.2016.2545928). The pipeline applies pre-emphasis, Hamming-windowed spectra, normalized gammatone-shaped power bands, a five-frame medium-time average, asymmetric background estimation, temporal masking, frequency smoothing, online mean-power normalization, a 1/15 power compression, and an orthonormal DCT-II. No external feature library or trained model is loaded.

The five-frame average is aligned with its middle short-time power frame, adding two analysis hops of delay. Negative-time history is zero. Startup outputs zero until three frames exist. The earliest result therefore occurs after frameTime plus two hopTime intervals. Band/noise estimates continue adapting during silence; a zero-power central frame yields zero cepstra. Results hold between hops.

The medium-time observation spans frameTime plus four hopTime intervals. Changing hopTime changes that observation span; exponential background and peak time constants are adjusted separately. The analytic filter bandwidth convention follows Hohmann (2002), described in link::https://amtoolbox.org/amt-1.6.0/doc/modelstages/hohmann2002_filter_code.php::.

This implementation uses the sampled squared response of the Hohmann analytic four-stage gammatone model, rather than the Slaney real-filter frontend in the paper. The power reference initializes from the first positive processed frame instead of a training-corpus mean. Forgetting factors are rescaled from their published 10 ms update interval. These conventions and a separately implemented numerical oracle are documented in the PNCC implementation report. It is not a numerical clone of the authors' speech-recognition frontend; no speech-recognition accuracy claim is made. Utterance-level cepstral mean subtraction and derivative features are not implicit; FeatureDelta and FeatureDeltaDelta can process the output when needed.

Like vanilla MFCC, PNCC summarizes spectral shape as cepstral coefficients. Its auditory bands, noise suppression, temporal masking and power compression give it different adaptation behavior and additional latency.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: in
Mono audio-rate input.
argument:: numCoeffs
Literal output count, 1 through 64, not exceeding the available band/DCT orders.
argument:: numBands
Literal gammatone band count, 4 through 128, default 40.
argument:: minFreq
Literal lower center frequency in Hz, nonnegative, default 200.
argument:: maxFreq
Literal upper center frequency in Hz, greater than minFreq, default 8000. Limited to 0.45 of the sample rate.
argument:: frameTime
Literal Hamming-window duration in seconds, 0.005 through 0.1, default 0.0256. Rounded up to samples; configurations exceeding 8192 samples are rejected. The FFT is the next power of two at least twice the window size.
argument:: hopTime
Literal analysis interval in seconds, 0.001 through 0.1, default 0.01. Rounded to samples independently of the server block size.
argument:: preEmphasis
Coefficient in x[n] - coefficient*x[n-1], 0 through 1, default 0.97. Zero bypasses pre-emphasis.
argument:: include0
Literal positive value includes coefficient zero; zero begins with coefficient one.
argument:: smoothBands
Literal radius of the neighborhood averaging the noise-suppression transfer function, 0 through numBands - 1. Default 4.
returns::
Array of numCoeffs cepstral values. These are dimensionless features; higher coefficients can be negative.

examples::
PNCC extracts Power-Normalized Cepstral Coefficients designed for noise-robust speech and audio analysis via medium-time spectral averaging, asymmetric background noise suppression, and power-law compression. In this example, 13 PNCC coefficients modulate a dual-oscillator vocal formant synth: the synthesized timbre shadows the saxophone melody with high immunity to background room noise. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.bars:: displays the 13 power-normalized cepstral coefficients in real time.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 13);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var pncc = PNCC.kr(source, numCoeffs: 13, numBands: 32, minFreq: 80, maxFreq: 8000, include0: 1);
        var c1 = pncc[1].lag(0.04);
        var c2 = pncc[2].lag(0.04);

        // Vocal formant synthesizer driven by PNCC
        var cutoff = c1.linlin(-15, 15, 250, 4000).clip(150, 6000);
        var q = c2.linlin(-10, 10, 0.15, 0.8).clip(0.1, 0.9);
        var synthVoice = BPF.ar(Saw.ar([110, 220]), cutoff, q).sum * 0.25;
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + synthVoice;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, pncc) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.bars("PNCC Coefficients", ~mir2Bus, 13, -25.0, 25.0);
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

